The exponential rise in the need for better, more efficient power sources has sparked an incredible amount of research into batteries. A primary focus of research has been increasing the energy density of batteries, as it allows for lighter, more portable storage of energy. Lithium-ion batteries, for example, have a much higher energy density than conventional lead-acid batteries and can be used for various purposes, such as in electric vehicles.
## WILEY
Li-ion batteries (LIBs) have wide applications owing to their high-energy density and stable cycle characteristics. Nevertheless, with the rapid expansion of electric vehicle market, issues such as explosion of LIBs and the need to secure a longer driving distance have emerged. In this work, functional metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are introduced as a separator in LIBs, in which a highly heat-resistant polymer separator is fabricated through electrospinning. The MOFs can scavenge impurities (including gas, water, and hydrofluoric acid) that positively affect battery performance and safety. The multi-functional separator suppresses salt decomposition when a nickel-rich cathode is operated at high voltage and high temperature through it. This delays the deterioration of the cathode interface and results in a superb cycle stability with $75\%$ retention even in the presence of 500 ppm of water in the electrolytes. In addition, the pouch cell is manufactured by enlarging the separator, and the degree of electrode swelling due to gas generation and interface degradation in the pouch state is alleviated to $50\%$ or less. These findings highlight the necessity of scavenging impurities to maintain excellent performance and provides the development direction of functional separators in LIBs.
## 1. Introduction
Lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) are widely used in electric vehicles,[1–3] portable electronic devices,[4] and energy storage systems,[5] owing to their high-energy density, high operating voltage, and excellent rate capability.[6] Many researchers have intensively studied LIBs, given the burgeoning growth of interests in energy storage devices. However, there is a lack of clarity regarding capacity fading and stability of LIBs during repeated battery cycles. In organic electrolytes, the conventional lithium salts, $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ , actively react with a trace amount of water, resulting in decomposition and parasitic reactions, as shown in Equation (1).[7–9]
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\left(\mathrm{s}\right)+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{l}\right)\rightarrow\mathrm{LiF}\left(\mathrm{s}\right)}\\ &{+\mathrm{POF}_{3}\left(\mathrm{g}\right)+2\mathrm{HF}\left(\mathrm{g}\right),\mathrm{POF}_{3}\left(\mathrm{g}\right)}\\ &{+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{l}\right)\rightarrow\mathrm{POF}\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\left(\mathrm{g}\right)+2\mathrm{HF}\left(\mathrm{g}\right)}\end{array}
$$
This reaction leads to profound side effects. Highly corrosive HF gas induces transition metal dissolution, electrode erosion, and solid electrolyte interface (SEI) damage.[10–12] These intractable problems severely degrade the overall performance of the battery cell. The transition metal dissolution produces a superoxide radical $(\mathsf{O}_{2}^{\bullet-})$ that decomposes the ethylene moiety, a key solvent in electrolyte, producing CO, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .[13–15] These gases increase the pressure inside the battery cell, affecting battery safety. In addition, the continuous consumption of electrolytes causes capacity fading, which in turn shortens battery lifespan. Moreover, the continuous formation of ionic insulating byproducts, such as $\mathsf{P O F}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{POF}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})$ , and ${\mathrm{POF}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ , severely affect the impedance increase of battery cells. This is because these byproducts are deposited on the anode surface as SEI components.[16] The produced LiF is accumulated on the cathode and impedes ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ migration, reducing Li-ion conductivity.[17,18]
In recent years, various strategies have been exploited to mitigate the above-mentioned issues. One widely used method is to coat the cathode surface with inorganic materials (e.g., metal oxides, metal fluorides, and metal phosphates) and organic materials (e.g., carbon structures, polymers, and sugars).[19–25] The coating layers prevent an HF attack to a certain extent, enhancing cathode stability. However, it is difficult to control the thickness and uniformity of the coating layer, and the peril of residual byproducts and HF gas is unavoidable.[26]
In this context, research on additives for scavenging water or HF has attracted much attention. Water or HF molecules can be captured by reaction with Lewis base sites such as phosphite, amino silane, and silyl ether.[27–30] Although a conscious choice of additive is an effective way to alleviate parasitic reaction related to decomposition of SEI and electrolytes, many concerns, such as high viscosity, side reaction, and floating byproducts, should be carefully considered.[31] Meanwhile, a strategy for introducing and fixing a scavenger to the separator has been recently explored. Zhou et al. used an in-built water scavenger within an metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)-based separator to realize the appreciable cycling performance for LIBs.[32] However, they analyzed MOF composites in environments that lacked significant occurrence of HF at electrodes with low loading density. In other words, direct evidence for gas reduction was scarcely investigated in the scavenging systems. MOFs are crystalline materials consisting of metal ions and organic ligands that have high porosity and diverse structures. Therefore, MOFs have wide applications in various areas, such as drug delivery, gas filtration, catalyst, and energy materials.[33–36] Nevertheless, it should be noted here that the strong bonds constituting the structure of MOFs can be easily destroyed by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and even more severely decomposed in acidic or basic conditions.[37]
Thus, in this study, we present an all-scavenging separator membrane by introducing a modified MOF to scavenge impurities (including water and HF) and reduce other gases, such as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO. First, we synthesized aminefunctionalized $\mathrm{zr}$ -based MOFs $\mathrm{[}\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(2$ -amino-1,4- benzenedicarboxylate) $_6]$ $(\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ ), which is highly porous and thermally/chemically stable. $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has a substantial absorption ability toward gas and water and exhibits a low $\alpha$ value (relative pressure at $50\%$ of ${\tt q}_{\mathrm{max.}}$ which is adsorption capacities in water adsorption test) of 0.22.[38] In addition, the functional group of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can improve ionic conductivity because it enables the interaction with $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and transport Liion.[39] Moreover, we conducted additional modification using a ring-opening reaction between the amino functional group and (3-glycidyloxypropyl)trimethoxy-silane (GPTMS), which has the ability to scavenge HF in electrolytes, to attain GPTMSfuctionalized UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (GU).[40,41] As a result, all-impurities scavenging separator was fabricated through an electrospinning process with polyacrylonitrile (PAN) polymer containing functional MOFs $\lvert\mathbf{G}\mathbf{U}@\mathbf{P}\mathbf{A}\mathbf{N}\rvert$ . The PAN has a high thermal stability than polyolefin separator, so it can suppress membrane shrinkage and delay thermal runaway. Water-containing ${\sqrt{500}}\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of water in electrolyte) cycle performance of LIBs having $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN separator was highly improved to $75\%$ after 200 cycles.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Fabrication of MOFs-Based Separator
$\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , named as UiO in this paper, having an average diameter of $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ , was synthesized via a solvothermal reaction of zirconium chloride and 2-aminoterephthalic acid in N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF) (Figure S1, Supporting Information)
according to a previous report.[42] Then, GPTMS was modified on the surface of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ through a ring-opening reaction. Poweder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis showed that the GU maintained the original structure of UiO (Figure 1a; Figure S2, Supporting Information). The Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectra of GU showed the absorption peak of silyl at $1070~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , indicating that silane groups were successfully introduced on the surface of UiO by the ring-opening reaction between epoxide and amino group (Figure S3, Supporting Information). Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) analysis confirmed the relative component ratio of UiO and GPTMS to be 0.26–0.74 (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The excess amount of GPTMS blocks the pores of UiO particles with the increase in the content of GPTMS; thus, it must be functionalized in an appropriate amount (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The surface-modified GU showed remarkable dispersibility in the PAN polymer solution, and a simple electrospinning process fabricated a flexible separator of $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ (Figure 1a). Figures 1b,c show evenly distributed GU in the nanofiber. By performing transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image mapping of $\mathrm{zr}$ and O elements, it was observed that the MOFs were uniformly distributed within the fiber. Furthermore, the mapping image of purple-colored Si element revealed that the silane group was effectively coated on the surface, as confirmed by the evenly dispersion of Si element (Figure 1d).
## 2.2. Impurity Scavenging Properties of MOFs
UiO is a highly porous and robust MOF. The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption– desorption isotherms were analyzed to examine the amount of reduction in the surface area after GPTMS functionalization. The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface areas of UiO and GU were measured to be 1171.6 and $919.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively (Figure S5, Supporting Information). The GU showed abundant adsorption sites and permanent porosity as a scavenger. Water vapor and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption experiments were conducted to investigate the affinity of MOFs with them (Figure 2a). Hydrophilicity of UiO was determined to be 0.22 based on $\alpha$ value, which is relative humidity at half of the total water uptake. The GU showed a similar hydrophilicity value of 0.23. The water vapor uptake amount of UiO and GU was found to be 0.44 and $0.36~\mathrm{cc~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake amount of UiO and GU was ${\approx}270\$ and $212{\mathrm{~cc~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively, at $196\mathrm{~K~}$ and 1.0 bar (Figure 2b). The MOF can capture moisture in their highly ordered cavity structure.[43] Nevertheless, MOFs composed of organic structures are easily decomposed due to HF generated from the decomposition of $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ in the electrolyte. Thus, it is important to prove the stability of GU for practical use as a scavenger. PXRD analysis was conducted to confirm the stability of UiO and GU in arbitrarily destroyed electrolytes. For this, $0.05\mathrm{~g~}$ of samples was immersed in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of electrolytes and 500 ppm of water for 5 days to generate HF. Most of the major peaks, such as (111) and (002), of UiO disappeared, as shown in Figure 2c. FT-IR analysis revealed the complete collapse of the complex structure, as confirmed by the disappearance of peaks at wavelengths of $1300{-}1600~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and the formation of broad OH peaks (Figure S6a, Supporing Information). However, GU is stable even in the destroyed electrolytes, owing to the HF scavenging ability of GPTMS. FT-IR analysis confirmed that SiF was formed by scavenging HF of the methoxy silane group (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). UiO has an excellent water adsorption amount of $0.44~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , but the salt decomposition reaction was accelerated by a small amount of HF formed as soon as the electrolytes reacted with water. The electrolytes were decomposed to $\mathrm{PO}_{2}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ and HF, which corresponds to the color change of $\mathsf{p H}$ paper (Figure 2d). Interestingly, the electrolytes containing GU scavenge both moisture and HF, maintaining the original structure of MOFs, showing no color change of $\mathsf{p H}$ paper (Figure 2e). $^{19}\mathrm{F}$ -NMR spectroscopy confirmed the degree of salt decomposition after the reaction with electrolytes containing MOFs and water. We may conclude that the HF $(63.1\mathrm{ppm})$ , produced due to the rapid reaction between water and electrolytes, decreased the structural stability of UiO. This finding is similar with HF $(74.2\ \mathrm{ppm})$ produced in the MOF-free pure liquid electrolytes $(74.2\mathrm{ppm})$ (Figures 2f,g). In contrast, the electrolytes containg GU did not form any salt decomposition products, such as $\mathrm{PO}_{2}\mathrm{F}_{2}$ and HF (Figure 2h). MOFs are known to be stable in some acids,[44] but its structural stability can be deteriorated by HF, phosphoric acid, and electrolyte decomposition products present in the electrolytes. Thus, this finding indicates that they can be considered for use as a battery material.
## 2.3. Characterization of Separators
The characteristics of a highly heat-resistant functional separator containing GU with excellent impurity scavenging properties and oxidation resistance were evaluated. A GU $@$ PAN separator was fabricated by combining PAN having excellent thermal stability with functional MOFs having excellent capturing abilities and oxidation resistance. The effect of GU trapped inside the fiber on gas capturing was examined by measuring the gas adsorption of evenly dispersed GU in the PAN fiber structure. In Figure S7 (Supporting Information), the adsorption degree of moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ further increased with the increase in MOF content. The flexibility of separator is essential to improve the safety and processability of battery cell assembly. When 40 wt. $\%$ GU was used in a polymer solution for electrospinning, the degree of elongation in the GU $@$ PAN separator membrane was reduced to $<10$ times compared to that of the pristine PAN membrane (Figure S8, Supporting Information). Thus, the $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN separator membrane containg 30 wt. $\%$ GU was chosen as the optimum condition, considering its gas adsorption and mechanical properties. The $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN composite separator has adsorption capacity and affinity toward water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ despite the use of a small amount of GU; meanwhile, polyethylene (PE) and PAN hardly captured water and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 3a,b). The multi-functional $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ separator membrane showed good scavenging properties for $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(100.3\mathrm{cc}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas $(33\mathrm{\cc\{g}^{-1}})$ . It physically separates both electrodes (anode and cathode) and provides a passage for the movement of lithium ions. It also increases battery resistance during the slow movement of lithium ions in the separator.[45] Electrospun separators have many pores and specific surface area because 1D nano-sized fibers overlap to form a 3D layer.[46] The Gurley values of PAN and $\mathbf{GU}@\mathbf{PAN}$ were remarkably low at 0.87 and 0.56, respectively, due to the low tortuosity characteristics of the electrospun separator (Figure 3c). In addition, electrospun separator has seven times higher ionic conductivity than the commercial PE separator due to its high wettability with electrolytes (Figure 3d; Figure S9, Supporting Information). Chronoamperometry and EIS measurements were conducted to evaluate the Li-ion mobility, and the results showed that the $\mathrm{GU}@\mathrm{PAN}$ had higher Liion transference number of 0.603 compared to PE. The anchoring effect of MOFs appears to enhance the mobility of Li- ions (Figure S10, Supporting Information).[47] Ensuring the oxidation stability of separator is crucial for reliable high voltage battery performance. To this end, we conducted a linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) test to evaluate the stability of the GU $@$ PAN membrane. Our results showed that the current value of $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN was lower than that of PE at $5\mathrm{~V~}$ or higher, indicating its superior oxidation stability (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The separators physically prevent both electrodes from contacting each other, but thermal runaway of the battery can be accelerated when the separator shrinks in an ignition situation. Therefore, the heat resistance property of the separator is important and was confirmed by measuring the thermal contraction rate. Multi-functional GU $@$ PAN has a high thermal stability than the olefin separator at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After 1 h of storage, the olefin membrane exhibited a thermal shrinkage of ${>}50\%$ , while the GU $@$ PAN maintained its original shape (Figure 3e). When the temperature increased up to $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , PE completely decomposed, but the PAN-based separators did not shrink. Since it maintains ${>}80\%$ of the area even at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , it can significantly delay the thermal runaway phenomenon. (Figure S12, Supporting Information). Moreover, the heat resistance property was confirmed by directly setting the separator fire. PE shrinks quickly after 2 s, but, in the case of GU $@$ PAN, shrinkage hardly occurs even after $10\mathrm{{s}}$ due to its high resistance to heat (Figure 3f).
## 2.4. Electrochemical Performance of GU@PAN
All-impurities scavenging-GU $@$ PAN separators were further investigated in $4.4\mathrm{~V~}$ cut-off voltage LIBs. The battery cell was composed of $\mathrm{LiNi}_{0.8}\mathrm{Co}_{0.1}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (NCM811) as the cathodes and graphite as the anode within a voltage range between 2.7–4.4 V (Figure 4a). The initial charge and discharge proceeded slowly at $0.1~\mathrm{C}$ , and specific capacity and initial Coulombic efficiency of $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ $(213.3\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , $88.5\%)$ were similar to those of PE $(212\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $87.9\%$ ). However, when the rate performance of discharge was evaluated at room temperature, the discharge rate of $3\mathrm{~C~}$ showed a dramatic capacity difference; the capacity was more than five times higher even at a discharge rate of $10\mathrm{~C~}$ (Figure 4b). This is attributed to the high ionic conductivity and air permeability of $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ . Functional separators can be used to develop batteries for electric vehicles as they have the potential to increase the capacity and reduce the charging time. To determine the feasibility of practical battery applications, NCM//graphite full cell test was conducted under extreme conditions to evaluate the impurity scavenging ability of GU $@$ PAN. In a nickel-rich battery system, continuous salt decomposition occurs in the presence of moisture, and the cathode interfacial undergoes severe deterioration at high temperatures.[48] The battery full cell using PE did not operate from 120 cycles at a constant voltage of 4.4 V due to the continuous decomposition reaction between the electrode interface and electrolytes (Figure 4c; Figure S13 Supporting Information). The $\mathrm{GU}@\mathrm{PAN}$ separator suppressed battery deterioration by trapping HF generated by salt decomposition and moisture in the electrolytes, exhibiting a capacity retention of $75\%$ after 100 cycles. In the initial cycle, the bulk resistance (0.7 Ohm) of $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN-incorporated cells is lower than that of PE (1.1 Ohm) owing to its high Li-ion conductivity, and the overall interfacial resistance values are similar. As the cycle proceeds, the serous interfacial side reaction takes place on the battery electrode using PE due to impurities produced in the electrolytes, and the resistance increases more than 10 times higher than that of GU@PAN (33 Ohm) (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the effect of varying GU content on the separator performance was investigated. As the amount of GU increased, the gas adsorption capacity and HF capture capacity of the separator improved, leading to an overall improvement in battery performance. Specifically, after 200 cycles, the capacity retention of $30\%$ MOF $(60.7\%)$ was much improved than that of $10\%$ MOF $(55.6\%)$ (Figure S15, Supporting
Information). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) depth profiles of the electrode interphase of an overcharged battery after a high-temperature cycle showed the accumulation of the decomposition byproducts of Li salts and carbonate byproducts at the cathode interface having a thickness of ${\approx}200~\mathrm{nm}$ due to oxidative decomposition of the electrolytes (Figure 4d). However, in the full cell using $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ , minimal salt decomposition and a thin layer of cathode-electrolyte-interphase (CEI) werewas observed on the cathode surface (Figure 4e). With the increase in the resistance of PE-incorporated cells, the structural stability of the secondary particles of NCM decreases, numerous cracks are formed, and additional side reactions occur at the fractured CEI interface (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The collapse of the cathode structure due to the destroyed CEI layer enabled the dissolved metal ions to move to the cathode and destroy the anode interphase.[49] Surface analysis of separators was performed by SEM after cycle. The PE separator was blocked with foreign substances that desorbed from the electrode and produced by the reaction of cathode and HF. This may explain why it is difficult to proceed with the cycle further. In contrast, the GU $@$ PAN maintained the large pores even after 200 cycles, and the GU particles were also well preserved. In the case of a full cell using PE, ICPOES analysis confirmed that a large amount of Ni $(2762~\mathrm{ppm})$ was deposited on the graphite, significantly affecting cycle degradation. In the case of a full cell using $\mathbf{G}\cup\ @\mathbf{P}\mathrm{AN}$ , the interfacial side reaction was suppressed through the scavenging of impurities, and the dissolution amount $(400\ \mathrm{ppm}$ of Ni) was greatly reduced (Figure S18, Supporting Information). In order to ensure effective battery operation at a wide temperature range, it is important to consider the impact of low temperature on the battery performance. The GU $@$ PAN exhibited superior ionic conductivity compared to PE, resulting in improved capacity and stable cycle performance at ${\boldsymbol{\cdot}}10^{\circ}{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C}}}$ (Figure S19, Supporing Information).
## 2.5. Safety Characteristic of GU@PAN
In LIBs, abnormal electrochemical reactions can cause thermal runaway by increasing battery resistance. In addition, the impurities in the batteries cause interfacial side reactions, which generate gas, greatly inflate the battery, and increase the risk of explosion.[50] Functional GU@PAN separators can suppress many factors that affect battery safety (including metal dissolution and salt decomposition), and this can be prevented through $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and HF scavenging. Moreover, the separator, including GU, can adsorb gas, thus, improving battery safety (Figure 5a).[51] In situ differential electrochemical mass spectroscopy (DEMS) confirmed the amount of gas generated during the battery operation, and the amount of $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas generated by the side reaction between the initially formed interphase and moisture was reduced. In the GU $@$ PAN separator, the amount of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas generated due to the decomposition between the cathode interface and electrolyte at a high voltage of $4.4\mathrm{V}$ was found to be reduced (Figure 5b; Figure S20, Supporting Information).[52,53] Also, CO gas was detected in the PE separator during the initial cycle in the destroyed electrolytes with $500\mathrm{ppm}$ of water (Figure 5c).
The addition of 500 ppm water in the electrolytes significantly decomposed lithium salts, which subsequently generated HF via its reaction with water. CO gas was also generated because HF accelerates the ring-opening reaction along with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ production reaction during the initial EC reductive decomposition (Figure S21, Supportng Information). The $\mathrm{GU}@\mathrm{PAN}$ greatly reduced the amount of produced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by suppressing the initial CO gas generation and severe electrolyte decomposition at high voltage and high temperature, exhibiting its excellent impurity scavenging ability $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , HF, and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ).[54–56] The progress of interface destruction in the pouch battery continuously genertated various gases. Subsequently, the cell swelling becomes extreme, which can cause the battery included in the actual electronic device to swell and pose a danger to consumers. The degree of cell swelling through the introduction of the GU $@$ PAN separator was investigated by fabricating single-electrode pouch cells and measuring their thickness. As a result, the initial cell thickness was found to be different due to the difference in separator thickness of PE and GU $@$ PAN. However, with further progress of the cycle, the thickness of PE-incorporated cells was significantly increased $10\%$ after $1^{\mathrm{st}}$ cycle and $12\%$ after 100 cycles). In contrast, the pouch cell with the GU $@$ PAN separator helped to reduce cell swelling $4.6\%$ after 100 cycles) because of interfacial stabilization along with suppression of gas generation (Figure 5d). The multi-functional $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN separator was introduced into the pouch cell, and the battery was stably operated with the help of highly flexible separator even under bending motion (Figure S22, Supportng Information).
## 3. Conclusion
The GU@PAN separator membrane was fabricated using GPTMS-functionalized UiO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ materials via a simple electrospinning technique. General MOFs are easily destroyed by acids produced from salt decomposition. Nevertheless, functional MOFs (GU) can effectively adsorb moisture and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas that may exist in the battery cell. In addition, the alkoxy functional group introduced on the surface of UiOs acted as an HF scavenger, leading to structural integrity of UiO particles. When the functional MOFs was combined with PAN with superior mechanical flexibility, all-scavenging $\mathbf{GU}@$ PAN separator exhibited good thermal stability and showed less shrinkage than olefin separators at high temperature.Thus, the discharge capacity of $\mathbf{GU}@\mathbf{PAN}$ increases more than five times at a rate of $5\mathrm{C}$ compared to the PE separator due to its high ionic conductivity.The GU@PAN separator showed significantly improved capacity retention $75\%$ after 200 cycles) even at high temperature $(55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and $4.4\mathrm{~V~}$ cut-off voltage due to its excellent impurity scavenging properties. Finally, in situ DEMS analysis confirmed that the amount of gas generated during cell operation was remarkably reduced, and the swelling of the pouch cell was suppressed, demonstrating a safe and high-performance battery operation. Thus, the effective design of the functional separator may provide safer and stable battery operation at high temperature and $4.4\mathrm{V}$ cut-off voltage conditions.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials and Chemicals: All reagents were purchased and used without further purification. Zirconium(IV) chloride was purchased from Alfa Aesar. 2-amino-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid was purchased from TCI. GPTMS, DMF, and PANpolyacrylonitrile (molecular weight of $150~\mathrm{kg}~\mathsf{m o l}^{-1};$ ) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. $\mathsf{L i N i}_{0.8}\mathsf{C o}_{0.1}\mathsf{M n}_{0.1}$ (NCM811, L&F Co., Ltd.) and graphite (Sigma Aldrich) were used as the cathode and anode active materials, respectively. PVDF (KF1100, $\mathsf{M n}=768.8~\mathsf{k g}~\mathsf{m o l}^{-1}$ , polydispersity index $=2.94$ , KUREHA Chem.) was used as the polymeric binder in the electrode. PE having a thickness of $76~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ was used as reference separators. Also, the baseline electrolyte $(1~\mathsf{M}~\mathsf{L i P F}_{6})$ was dissolved in ethylene carbonate/ethylmethyl carbonate/dimethyl carbonate (EC/EMC/DMC, $1/1/1$ by weight) (Soulbrain Co., Ltd.) and was later treated with calcium hydride $(\mathsf{C a H}_{2}$ , Sigma Aldrich) for water molecule elimination to prevent hydrolysis of $\mathsf{L i P F}_{6}$ salt. After the formation of the electrolytes, the $\mathsf{p H}$ value was found to be nearly neutral, as confirmed by observing the pH paper.
Synthesis of UiO-66- $N H_{2}$ (UiO): UiO was prepared according to a previously reported method.[42] Zirconium(IV) chloride ( $(25.8\:\mathrm{mg},0.71\:\mathrm{mmol})$ and 2-amino terephthalic acid ( $\mathsf{14.5~m g}$ , $0.08~\mathsf{m m o l}$ ) were mixed with $70\mathsf{m L}$ DMF in a vial $20\mathsf{m L}$ capacity), and then $1.372\mathsf{m L}$ of acetic acid was added to it. The vial was sealed and placed in an oven at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then the contents were cooled down to room temperature, and precipitates were obtained by centrifugation. After washing with DMF, the solid was washed with water and methanol. Finally, the UiO nanocrystals were dried in vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $3h$ .
Synthesis of $C\cdot U i O\cdot G G\cdot N H_{2}$ $(G U)$ : First, 0.114 mmol of UiO was dispersed well in a $20\mathsf{m l}$ DMF solvent at nitrogen atmosphere. Subsequently, $0.684~\mathsf{m m o l}$ of GPTMS was added into the UiO solution and reacted at ${\mathsf{l}}{\mathsf{l}}0{\mathsf{\Omega}}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and then centrifuged three times to remove the residual chemical. The resulting yellowish powder was dried in a vacuum oven at $70^{\circ}C$ overnight.
Fabrication of MOF-Based separators: PAN of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{1.2g}}$ was added to $70\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF solvent and vigorously stirred at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $6\textup{h}$ so as to prepare 15 wt. $\%$ of PAN solution. Then $0.078\mathrm{g}$ of GU ( $30\mathrm{wt.\%}$ in PAN) was added to $_{2\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $13\mathrm{\:wt.\%}$ of PAN solution to prepare GU $@$ PAN solution. Four different types of polymer solutions (10–40 wt. $\%$ of GU) were loaded into a syringe for the electrospinning process using an electrospinning machine (ESR200R2, NanoNC) on a silicon wafer ( $75c m$ in diameter) with the following conditions: an applied voltage of $15.0\mathsf{k V},$ a flow rate of $0.5~\mathsf{m L h^{-1}}$ , and a distance of $15c m$ between the syringe tip and a current collector. The as-spun PAN and $\mathsf{G U}@$ PAN were then dried at $70^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\ h$ to remove the residual solvent.
Fabrication of Electrode: The NCM811 electrode consisted of NCM, Super P, and PVDF binder in a weight ratio of 90:5:5. The graphite electrode consisted of graphite, Super P, styrene–butadiene rubber/sodium slat of carboxymethyl cellulose binder in a weight ratio of 95.5:1:5/1.5. The active loading of the cathode and anode was 13.6 and $10.3~\mathsf{m g}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , respectively, and the areal specific capacity was ${\approx}2.8~\mathsf{m A}\ h~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ . All electrodes were dried at $\mathsf{l}\mathsf{l}0^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ in vacuum before cell assembly, and the full cell was designed with a negative to positive capacity $(\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{P})$ ratio of 1.3.
Preparation of Destroyed UiO Solution and Destroyed GU Solution for Scavenging Properties Test: Two kinds of solutions were prepared to analyze the ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and HF scavenging properties of UiO and GU. Destroyed UiO/GU solution was prepared by mixing $5~\mathsf{m l}$ of fresh electrolyte (1 M LiPF6 EC/EMC/DMC $(1/1/1$ by weight)) and 0.5 wt.% UiO/GU, and 500 ppm of water was added to it. Each solution was mixed in a glove box and stored for 5 days.
Characterization: PXRD patterns were recorded on a Bruker D2 phaser diffractometer at $30~\mathsf{\ k V}$ and 10 mA for Cu $\upkappa\alpha$ $(\lambda\:=\overset{\cdot}{7}.54050\:\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ ) with a step size of $0.02^{\circ}$ in 2𝜃. The samples were evacuated $<10^{-5}$ mbar) at ${150~^{\circ}C}$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then ${\sf N}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ sorption isotherms of samples were obtained using a BELSORP-max at 77, 196, and $298\ k,$ respectively. The specific surface area was determined in the relative pressure range from 0.05 to 0.3 of the Brunauer–Emmett– Teller plot, and the total pore volume was calculated from the amount adsorbed at a relative pressure of 0.98–0.99. Thermogravimetric analysis was performed under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere at a scan rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ using a Q50 from TA instruments. The chemical composition of G-UiO was analyzed by ICP-OES (700-ES, Varian). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were taken using Hitach High-Technologies Cold FE-SEM operating at $\mathsf{10~k V.}$ TEM images were recorded on a JEOL JTEM-2100. Universal pH paper (UNIV) was used to investigate the $\mathsf{p H}$ value of electrolytes. The electrolytes were measured by $\boldsymbol{^{19}\mathsf{F}}$ -NMR spectroscopy $(470~\mathsf{M H z}$ , Bruker AVANCE 3 Ascend 500) using a coaxial-type tube to investigate the electrolyte decomposition reactions. The electrolytes were stored in a polytetrafluoroethylene tube (BK Instrument Inc.) to prevent the chemical reaction between HF and glass, and $T H F-d_{8}$ $(99.5\%$ , NMR grade, Sigma Aldrich.) with 1 wt. $\%$ hexafluorobenzene $(\mathsf C_{6}\mathsf F_{6}$ , $99.5\%$ , NMR grade, Sigma–Aldrich) was used as an internal standard for $^{19}\mathsf{F}$ -NMR analysis. The air permeability of the separator membrane was measured via a Gurley densometer (4110N, Gurley), and the ionic conductivity was obtained by impedance measurements and calculated by the following Equation (2):
$$
\sigma=\frac{d}{A\times R_{b}}
$$
where d denotes the thickness of the separator membrane (16 and $50\upmu\mathrm{m}$ ), $\mathsf{R}_{\mathsf{b}}$ is the bulk resistance and A denotes the area of stainless-steel electrodes $(7.76\ c m^{2})$ ). The shrinkage size of the separator membrane was calculated after being heated at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ (original size: $2\times2c m^{2}$ ) in order to measure the thermal stability of the separator membrane. The electrode surface was characterized by ex situ XPS (Scientific K-Alpha system, Thermo Scientific) with $\mathsf{A l}\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation $(\mathsf{h}\nu=7486.6\mathsf{e V})$ in ultrahigh vacuum. XPS spectra were calibrated by a hydrocarbon peak at a binding energy of 284.8 eV.
Electrochemical Characterization: The NCM//graphite full cell was galvanostatically cycled between 4.4 and $2.7\:\forall$ using a computer-controlled battery measurement system (WonATech WBCS 3000) in order to investigate the rate performance of each separator. The formation cycle was performed at a rate of $C/70$ in a constant-temperature drying oven at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Discharge rate performance was examined at different discharge rates of 2/C, 1, 3, 5, 7, $\mathsf{l o}\mathsf{C}$ with a fixed rate of charge (1 C). The destroyed electrolytes were mixed with 500 ppm of deionized water for 1 min, and then a coin-type full cell was assembled using the destroyed electrolytes to investigate the impurity scavenging performance at $55^{\circ}C$ . Fresh electrolyte of $\mathsf{l o o}\upmu\mathsf{l}$ was filled in a coin full cell. A formation cycle was conducted at ${\mathsf C}/{\mathsf{70}}$ , and subsequent cycles were proceeded at a rate of $\mathsf C/2$ . Then, high-temperature cycle test $(55^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ was evaluated at a rate of 1 C between 4.4 and $2.7\:\vee$ using the destroyed electrolytes. Low temperature test was examined at different discharge rates of 0.5, 1, 3, 5, $\textsf{7C}$ with a fixed rate of charge $(\l)\subset\l $ at $\cdot10\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The resistance of fullcells was obtained by measuring the EIS in the $10^{6}$ to ${\mathsf{10^{-2}}}{\mathsf{H z}}$ frequency range. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) on SS//Li cells was conducted to analyze the oxidation stability of various electrolytes under a scan rate of $\mathsf{I m V s^{-1}}$ at a potential range up to $6\ensuremath{\vee}$ (vs $\mathsf{L i}/\mathsf{L i^{+}})$ .
Fabrication of Pouch Cells: The NCM811/separators/graphite pouch full cell $(\approx30{\mathsf{m A h}})$ was assembled at an argon-filled glove box. NCM811, graphite, and separator were prepared with the size of $3\times4$ , $3.2\times4.2$ , and $3.5\times4.5c m^{2}$ , respectively. The electrode with a welded tap was stacked, and then $250\upmu\upmu\up$ of fresh electrolyte was filled in a pouch cell that was fully wetted with electrolyte and sealed by a heat sealer. After aging for $\mathsf{10~h}$ , the NCM811/separator/graphite pouch cell was galvanostatically cycled between 4.4 and $2.7\mathrm{V}.$ Their formation cycle was performed with a rate of $C/70$ at $25^{\circ}C$ Subsequent cycling at $\rceil\mathsf{C}$ was proceeded at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . After the 1st, 10th, $50\mathrm{{th}}$ , and 100th cycles, the thickness of the pouch cell was measured using a thickness gauge (TESA $\mathsf{u}$ -hite guage).
In Situ DEMS Analysis: DEMS analysis was conducted to monitor the CO $(m/z=28)$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $(\mathsf{m}/z=44)$ ) gas evolution occurring during the 1st cycle (with a rate of $C/70$ at $55^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ) in NCM//graphite full cell incorporated with PE or GU $@$ PAN separator. In situ DEMS cells were assembled in an argon-filled glovebox, and the gas evolution in the cells was detected at an interval of 5 min.
